---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Card from '../components/Card.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';

const shipped = [
  'Sign up and sign in with email, Google or Notion',
  'Age band gate and role selection',
  'Student and teacher profiles',
  'Teacher search and learning requests',
  'Learning relations with goals and tasks',
  'Evidence submission and feedback',
  'Structured messaging inside a relation',
  'Explainable progress and proof records',
  'Data export and account deletion',
  'Blocking and reporting',
];

const building = [
  'Realtime message delivery (currently polled)',
  'In-app uploads for avatars and evidence attachments',
  'In-app notifications for requests and feedback',
  'Offline and stale-data indicators',
  'Moderation tooling for reports',
];
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Node Learn is an early-access learning-relationship platform: what exists today, what is still being built, and the constraints we hold ourselves to."
>
  <Section
    eyebrow="About"
    title="A small product with a narrow, stubborn idea."
    lede="Node Learn began as a simple observation: the scarce thing in learning is not information, and never has been. It is a knowledgeable person paying attention to your specific work."
    narrow
  >
    <div class="space-y-5 text-body leading-7 text-foreground-secondary">
      <p>
        Almost every learning product answers a different question — how to distribute content more
        cheaply, how to keep someone in an app longer, how to compress a subject into short videos.
        Those are real engineering problems. They are not the problem that stops most people from
        learning something hard.
      </p>
      <p>
        What stops them is that nobody is going to look at what they made. You can read every article
        about writing and still not know why your paragraphs do not land. You can finish a
        programming course and still write code no one else can maintain. The gap closes when someone
        who knows the subject reads your work and tells you precisely what to change.
      </p>
      <p>
        So we built the smallest thing that makes that exchange durable: a shared space between one
        learner and one teacher, holding the goal they agreed on, the tasks it broke into, the work
        that was submitted and the feedback it received. We call it a
        <strong class="font-semibold text-foreground">learning relation</strong>, and it is the only
        object in the product that really matters.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything else follows from refusing to compromise that. No catalogue, because a catalogue
        sells access rather than attention. No streaks, because we do not want you here out of habit.
        No verification badges, because we have not verified anyone and would rather say so.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    eyebrow="Honest status"
    title="What exists today."
    lede="Node Learn is an early-access MVP. The core loop runs end to end; several things are visibly unfinished. Here is the real list."
    narrow
  >
    <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <Card class="border-l-4 border-l-success">
        <h3 class="text-h3 font-semibold text-foreground">Working now</h3>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2">
          {
            shipped.map((s) => (
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5 text-small leading-6 text-foreground-secondary">
                <svg class="mt-1 size-4 shrink-0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                  <path
                    d="M3.5 8.5 L6.5 11.5 L12.5 5"
                    class="stroke-success"
                    stroke-width="1.6"
                    stroke-linecap="round"
                    stroke-linejoin="round"
                  />
                </svg>
                <span>{s}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </Card>

      <Card class="border-l-4 border-l-warning">
        <h3 class="text-h3 font-semibold text-foreground">Still being built</h3>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2">
          {
            building.map((s) => (
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5 text-small leading-6 text-foreground-secondary">
                <svg class="mt-1 size-4 shrink-0" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                  <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="6" class="stroke-warning" stroke-width="1.4" />
                  <path d="M8 5v3.5l2 1.5" class="stroke-warning" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" />
                </svg>
                <span>{s}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </Card>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-6 text-small leading-6 text-foreground-tertiary">
      We would rather list the gaps than let you discover them. If something below matters a lot to
      you, say so — it moves up the list.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Where it runs" title="One codebase, three platforms." narrow>
    <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <Card>
        <h3 class="text-h3 font-semibold text-foreground">iOS &amp; Android</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-small leading-6 text-foreground-secondary">
          Native apps from a single Expo and React Native codebase, so both platforms get the same
          features at the same time.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card>
        <h3 class="text-h3 font-semibold text-foreground">Web</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-small leading-6 text-foreground-secondary">
          The same application runs in the browser with a desktop layout — no reduced "mobile web"
          version and no second product to maintain.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card>
        <h3 class="text-h3 font-semibold text-foreground">Accessibility</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-small leading-6 text-foreground-secondary">
          WCAG 2.2 AA is the design floor: 44px touch targets, visible focus, screen-reader labels,
          Dynamic Type and reduced-motion support.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section narrow class="pb-24">
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-8 shadow-card">
      <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold text-foreground">Tell us where we are wrong</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-body leading-7 text-foreground-secondary">
        The principles on this site are meant to be held to. If you find a place where the product
        contradicts them, that is a bug worth reporting.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a
          href="/contact"
          class="inline-flex min-h-touch items-center rounded-DEFAULT bg-primary px-6 text-body font-semibold text-on-primary transition-colors duration-150 hover:bg-primary-pressed"
        >
          Contact us
        </a>
        <a
          href={`mailto:${SITE.email.hello}`}
          class="inline-flex min-h-touch items-center rounded-DEFAULT border border-border-strong bg-surface px-6 text-body font-semibold text-foreground transition-colors duration-150 hover:bg-element"
        >
          {SITE.email.hello}
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>
</Base>

